i managed to find a copy of neil  degrasse tyson's phd thesis and i spent  all week trying to understand it but it  wasn't easy to read because it's 370  pages long and theses are not intended  to be read by anyone but experts on the  subject so i became an expert on  galaxies obtaining a phd is the highest  academic qualification for someone  wanting to pursue a career in science  it's been a few years now since i left  my own phd to pursue other things  looking at tyson's success i thought  that his career path might have been  more of a straight line to get to such a  level so imagine my surprise when i read  that this thesis was not his first  attempt at a phd  his line was indeed more squiggly  he started as a graduate student at the  university of texas but felt that the  expectation to dedicate all of his time  to research meant that he had to give up  his other interests and the rest of who  he was  and some of the advice some of the other  advisors thinking they're doing me a  favor said you know you have this  personality why don't you become a  computer salesman i know some people who  work for computers you could be they're  trying to do me a favor by getting me  out of their community i said to myself  if i'm going to have to do a whole new  phd  it's not going to be at this institution  it's got to be fresh i might as well be  fresh in another place i want to read  his thesis to see what kind of  scientific foundation he had before  launching himself into a career in  science communication  to see for myself his contributions to  scientific research and to test myself  to see if i stand a chance at  understanding any of it for his second  attempt at a phd this time at columbia  university tyson took a stab at  understanding why the center of our  galaxy is the way it is  his thesis has the title a study of the  abundance distributions along the minor  axis of the galactic bulge it was  published in 1992  when he was 33 a phd thesis or a  dissertation is an account of years of  intense scientific research and it must  contain an original contribution to  human knowledge so what was tyson's well  let's have a look  chapter 1 opens with the quote it is  important not to worship what is known  but to question it  tyson then describes the galactic bulge  as a remarkable compact zoo of stellar  populations  let's draw a map of our galaxy the milky  way  this is a side on view with many stars  in the spiral arms of the disc  around the galactic center there is a  bulge with a high concentration of stars  this is tyson's zoo  perhaps the main attraction is a black  hole at the very center  astronomers can peer in at the black  hole our galaxy's fierce lion but only  from afar  there's a fear of being eaten if we ever  got too close but the sun and therefore  our solar system and us are out here  nice and safe held by the spiral arms  out there we have the halo comprised of  older stars and globular clusters  tightly bound groups of many stars  here's tyson's sketch of what he did  which is that he looked into the  galactic bulge along seven different  lines of sight to understand the  populations of stars in the zoo and how  they change as you get further from the  center  the chosen lines of sight are low  extinction windows through the plane of  the disk  so he looked through sections of space  where there wasn't so much dust and gas  in the way to obscure the stars these  are images of the seven windows and you  can notice the steep drop in star  density from the innermost bulge to the  outermost halo each window is named with  its latitude the smaller numbers are  looking further out towards the sparse  halo tyson measured properties of these  stars like their chemical abundance and  velocities to answer the following  questions  what is the abundance distribution  function at different galactic latitudes  within the bulge is there a smooth  transition in abundance between the  bulge and the spheroid or halo if not at  what latitude does the transition occur  and was the bulge's primordial gas used  completely in star formation or was it  expelled by ongoing supernova driven  winds  to measure the chemical abundance of the  stars tyson used the ciro tololo  inter-american observatory located in  chile  to get time observing on the telescope  he would have had to submit a proposal  maybe even a year in advance and compete  against all the other astronomers  wanting a turn  then he had to hope that on his  allocated days the sky wouldn't be  cloudy because if it is he won't see  anything  chile was chosen to house many  observatories because it is dry and with  little light pollution but every  observing run is still a roll of the die  tyson's first observing run was for one  night on the 7th of june 1989  to get to the telescopes is a long drive  up a bumpy scary road to an altitude of  2200 meters  up there the oxygen level is so low that  you might be out of breath just walking  around  so why would he go all that way for just  one night  perhaps he hoped to observe for longer  but got bad weather all i can tell from  his thesis is that he observed four  fields of stars that night and returned  a year later for two more nights to get  the rest tyson collected information  about his stars in the bulge and  processed that data to find their  colours temperatures magnitudes and also  their chemical compositions  he used the washington photometric  system to measure certain wavelengths of  starlight and look for absorption lines  these lines indicate that some of the  light that left the hot interior of the  star was absorbed by atoms or ions in  the star's outer atmosphere  some photons will be blocked from  reaching us and their absence reveals  what elements the stars are made of  it's not all smooth sailing though there  are plenty of errors in every step of  the process to account for  for tyson these included overcrowding of  stars in his inner windows causing some  of the light profiles to overlap  there was also the difficult job of  removing foreground stars from the  images  these both gave uncertainties in the  final measurements  there are many pages of data math and  nuanced details but tyson eventually  arrived at abundance distributions  which he said is the principal focus of  the study doing science means making  incremental changes to models of how  things work  tyson noted some existing models of how  galaxies chemically evolve and wanted to  see if his newly collected data from the  bulge matched any of these models  the simplest model starts off with a  ball of gas and all it can do is turn  into stars  no gas escapes  this is called the closed box model and  if you were to plot the metal content of  the stars against the number of stars  you would get a plot shaped like this  this is the abundance distribution  in astronomy a metal is any element  that's not hydrogen or helium  that's not how chemists would define  metals but in astronomy it allows an  easy distinction between elements like  hydrogen and helium that have existed  since the birth of the universe and  heavier elements that must have been  made by stars  stars make metals through nuclear fusion  in their cores  or in supernova explosions  supernovae release metals back into the  interstellar medium the space between  the stars  from which new stars with higher  metallicities may form  a variant of the closed box is the leaky  box which allows some gas to escape out  into the rest of the galaxy the  abundance distribution for a leaky box  would be the same shape but shifted to  the left the stars have less metal in  them on average than stars in a closed  box because those metal rich gas  explosions from supernovae are escaping  so let's take a look at tyson's  abundance distributions here's the first  of his seven fields the one closest to  the galactic center  he measures metallicity as the ratio of  iron to hydrogen and says there is  remarkable agreement with the abundance  distribution expected from the simple  model or a closed box he fits a  theoretical curve with a yield of 1.25  which is a measure of how much metal is  being returned to the interstellar  medium  the x-axis is scaled to the abundance of  the sun so zero would be the sun and one  is ten times the sun's abundance  going out further from the galactic  centre his next plot has the same shape  but is shifted slightly to the right the  error bars are quite large and he says  that this also fits the simple model but  with a higher yield  the third plot moving further out again  has a higher average abundance but he  concludes that so far the region from  the galactic center to  0.75 kilopasca  is a well mixed closed box  a parsec is a unit of distance but if  you're more familiar with light years  then this closed box region is about 5  000 light years across  the next few fields show a shift to the  left and a drop in average abundance we  might be seeing that the box is now  leaking or there's a more complicated  process at hand  but there are also not many stars being  measured here the last field out in the  halo only has three stars this is them  so he doesn't really have a lot of  statistics on his side here  tyson's measurements constitute the  first direct measurement of the galactic  latitude dependence of iron to hydrogen  in the galactic bulge  he shows that it initially increases  away from the galactic centre then  steeply drops off as you leave the bulge  this drop off occurs around one kilopast  away from the centre leading him to  conclude that those stars within one  kiloparsec followed a common evolution  he also concludes that the inner bulge  was not significantly enriched by infall  of gas and that the inner bulge  underwent no catastrophic mass loss from  supernova-driven winds  he finds that the inner bulge is  chemically distinct from the halo while  the transition region is a blending of  the two  astrophysicists care about these  questions because any new information  about the state of our galaxy helps to  refine all the models which try to  explain what our galaxy is and how it  got here tyson talks about the  implications of his findings for models  of how clouds of gas may have collapsed  to form our galaxy in the first place  there has been a lot more work done on  galactic evolution since this was  written in the 90s large-scale surveys  done by satellite and ground-based  telescopes like gaia and apogee have  been able to create maps of millions of  stars and dwarf the three data points  that tyson had out in the halo astronomy  moves pretty fast since every year there  are upgraded tools and methods of  observing  this thesis isn't tyson's only  contribution to research he has 13  research papers which all will have  contributed some new understanding  some of his papers are taken from  chapters of this thesis and his failed  previous attempt and i'll link those  down in the description because the  entire thesis as a whole can only be  found through the university of columbia  his combined work has racked up a bit  over 2 000 citations instances where  other scientists have used it in some  way  whilst this isn't extremely high it's  pretty clear that neil degrasse tyson's  main contribution to science is not in  his research but in his work as a  communicator his early research work was  his introduction to all the key workings  of the subject we can also take a look  at his acknowledgements page he thanked  the scientists who helped him along the  way he thanked his supervisor mike rich  for his encouragement and attention to  scientific detail  and dedicated the work to his family  he ended his thesis with a final thought  i have lived and fulfilled a dream yet  my life is just beginning  most of the notes i took and the facts i  learned about galaxies never made it to  the screen in this video but i really  feel that for this week at least i did  become an expert on galaxies even though  i might forget it all next week i found  this thesis surprisingly good at  covering a base of topics that one would  need going forwards to work in astronomy  or its communication and i suppose  that's exactly the point of a thesis  but then success is all about what you  do from here  this portion of the video is kindly  sponsored by skillshare  skillshare is an online learning  community with thousands of online  classes where members come together to  find inspiration and take the next step  in their creative journey for example  there is this excellent class by ali  abdal covering video editing with final  cut pro the class could take you from  having no experience to editing great  youtube videos  final cut is the software that i use to  edit my videos and even though i've been  doing it for a few years now i still  learned a few new tips and shortcuts  from ali's class joining this class  through skillshare is also much cheaper  than many of the other expensive editing  courses out there  in fact it can be free for you there's a  special link down in the description and  the first 1000 people to click it will  get a one month free trial of skillshare  so thanks skillshare for supporting my  channel and for helping people to try  new things  also a thank you to my patreon  supporters including today's patreon can  of the day  you

